    "title": "Wildlife experts weigh-in on Coyote sightings - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Max Diekneite ,\n38 minutes ago\nST. LOUIS – Videos are popping up online recently of coyote sightings across the St. Louis area, including one early Tuesday morning in south St. Louis.\nWildlife officials say the sightings may become more common as we enter the fall season and temperatures drop.\nThe Wildlife Rescue Center tells FOX 2 fall is a time when young coyotes break off and start looking for territory of their own.\nThey explained that winter is the mating season for coyotes, and by the fall many of those young coyotes are ready to venture off on their own.\nThe Department of Conservation says coyotes can be found across the St. Louis region, including in the city in places like Forest Park and Tower Grove Park. They say the easiest way to keep coyotes away from your home is to avoid keeping food outside, and to put your trash out as close to pickup time as possible.\nThe department added that while coyotes rarely represent a threat to humans, they can be a serious risk for pets. They advise people to keep their pets indoors if a coyote is spotted nearby, especially at night.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to FOX 2.",
